# Bright Coffee Shop Case Study

### About the project
This project is about the Bright Coffee Shop sales data. The shop has daily transaction data but the business was struggling with sales. They appointed a new CEO who wants to grow revenue and improve product performance.

As a junior analyst I was asked to look at the historical data and find insights that can help the CEO make better decisions. I had to clean the data, analyze it and build a presentation.

### Problem Statement
The main problem was the data was not ready for analysis. The price column had 3,1 instead of 3.1, there was no total amount/revenue column, and we could not see peak hours. Without fixing this, the CEO cannot know which products make money or when to add more staff.

### Aim of the project
The aim was to clean and analyze the sales data to support the new CEO.

Objectives were:
- Find which products make the most revenue
- Find best performing times of the day
- Check sales trends by product and time
- Give recommendations to improve sales

### How I did it (Steps)
First I planned the project with a Gantt chart. I broke it into 4 weeks - from getting data, cleaning, analysis, to dashboard.

Then I did data cleaning in Databricks:
- I removed duplicates and checked for missing values in unit_price, transaction_qty and transaction_date
- I fixed the price issue from 3,1 to 3.1 using REPLACE and CAST to double
- I created total_amount = unit_price * transaction_qty for revenue
- I added day name and month name from transaction_date
- I created day classification (Weekend vs Weekday) and time classification (Rush hour, Mid Morning etc)
- I also did a 30-min time bucket to find peak times as required in the brief

For analysis I wrote SQL queries in Databricks (Query 1 to 11):
- Checked min and max dates, checked store locations (Lower Manhattan, Astoria, Hell's Kitchen)
- Checked different products and product categories
- Checked lowest and highest prices and daily transactions
- Then I made one long polished query with all the new columns for Excel

Lastly I did visualization in Excel. I made pivot tables and charts for revenue by product, sales by time and category. I used Canva for the final CEO presentation.

### Key Findings / Summary of Results
After cleaning, the data made more sense:

- Some products make more money than others. Coffee and Brewed Chai were top in revenue.
- Peak sales happen in the morning 8-9am and afternoon 4-6pm. Those rush hours are busy.
- Coffee category performs better than other categories.
- Sales change a lot depending on time of day. There is a slump after lunch.
- I also grouped customers into Low, Medium and High spend.

Overall the project helped to turn raw data into insights the CEO can use to increase revenue, like focusing promos during slow hours and staffing more people during rush.

